        and maximise it.

        Candidate generation (why it is complete):
        For each ordered pair of adjacent values (a, b) with a != b, x -> y
        "covers" that pair iff x in {a, b} and y in {a, b} (i.e. {x, y} = {a, b}).
        If x -> y is optimal and x never occurs next to y, then x -> y changes only
        pairs whose two endpoints are x (become y, could break) or pairs with exactly
        one x and one other value != y (become (y, other), never gain). Such a move
        cannot beat doing nothing... unless moving x to y creates equalities with y's
        that are adjacent to x's — contradiction with "x never next to y". Hence an
