        adjacent pair (a, b):
            a -> b and b -> b's neighbour  (both directions)
        plus the "global frequency" moves: let best = most frequent value, second =
        second most frequent distinct value; try v -> best for every value v != best,
        and best -> second (handles arrays where x never touches y).

        For each candidate (x, y) the gain is evaluated in O(number of pairs touching
        x or y) using per-value adjacency lists, so total work is O(sum of pair
        incidences) = O(n) across all candidates... To be safe we bound work per
        candidate: each candidate (x,y) is processed in O(deg(x)+deg(y)) where
        deg(v) = number of adjacent pairs touching value v. Sum over candidates of
        deg(x)+deg(y) = O(n) because each adjacent pair generates only O(1)
        candidates (4 directions per unordered adjacent pair, deduplicated).

        Details of gain computation for x -> y (x != y):
          base  = total equal adjacent pairs originally.
          For each index i with value in {x, y} look at neighbours j = i-1, i+1:
            after value of i = y (whether it was x or y).
            after value of j = y if nums[j] in {x,y} else nums[j].
          Count new equal pairs on those edges, subtract old contributions of those
          same edges, add to base. Each edge is examined from its endpoint(s); to
          avoid double counting we only count edge (i, i+1) once by iterating i over
